course: dictionary
lesson: 2841
title: DNA, the instructions inside you
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: DNA is a chemical found inside the cells of every living thing. Think of it as a set of instructions, like a recipe, that tells a living thing how to grow, how to work and what it looks like. «every living thing carries a recipe called DNA» is the key idea here.
explain: You get half your DNA from your mother and half from your father, so children often look like their parents. «which parts you get is a mix, so brothers and sisters differ, but identical twins share the same DNA.»
explain: Words can also be used in a different way. When people say something's «DNA», they mean its most basic part, like «kindness is in our school's DNA». Read the words around it to tell which meaning is used: a science sentence, or a sentence about people and what they are like.
fact: DNA stands for deoxyribonucleic acid, but everyone just says DNA.
fact: DNA is shaped like a twisted ladder called a double helix.
fact: DNA is found in the nucleus of a cell, and genes are small sections of it.
fact: DNA tests help doctors, help police solve crimes and help families find relatives.
fact: Stretched out, the DNA in one human cell is about two metres long, packed tightly into a tiny nucleus.
exercise: What does DNA carry? | Light for the leaves of a tree | Water for the cells of a plant | *Instructions for how a living thing grows, works and looks | Sounds for the ears of an animal
exercise: «You get half your DNA from your mother and half from your father.» Why do children often look like their parents? | Because they copy their parents' faces | Because parents buy them the same clothes | Because they live in the same house | *Because they get half their DNA from each parent
exercise: In «kindness is in our school's DNA», what does DNA mean? | *The most basic part of what the school is | A small chemical inside a cell | A test used by the police | A tiny ladder used in a lab
test: What does the word DNA stand for? | digital neural array | *deoxyribonucleic acid | do not never agree | dinosaur nucleus acid
test: Why do police use DNA? | To count the cells in a leaf | *To help solve crimes, using a hair or a drop of blood | To make food from light | To measure how fast an animal runs
exam: Which best describes DNA? | *A chemical inside the cells of living things that carries their instructions | A bone that holds the body up | A kind of light that glows in the dark | A type of rock found deep in the ground
